\title{A Coherently Invertible Map Is Contractible}
\taxon{Proposition}
\import{macros}
\p{
    Every [coherently invertible map](def-005K) is a
    [contractible map](def-005F). Explicitly, there is a function
    ##{
        \mathsf{isCohInv}(f)\to\mathsf{isContrMap}(f).
    }
}
\proof{
    Let #{f:A\to B} have coherent inverse data #{(g,G,H,K)}, and fix
    #{y:B}. We contract the fiber #{\mathsf{fib}_f(y)} around
    ##{
        c_y\defeq(g(y),G(y)).
    }
    Consider an arbitrary #{(x,p):\mathsf{fib}_f(y)}. An identity in the fiber
    from #{(g(y),G(y))} to #{(x,p)} is equivalently a pair consisting of
    ##{
        \alpha:g(y)=x
    }
    and a path asserting that the following triangle commutes:
    \graph{
        \begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large, row sep=large]
            {f(g(y))}
                \arrow[rr, "{\mathsf{ap}_f(\alpha)}"]
                \arrow[dr, "{G(y)}"']
            && {f(x)}
                \arrow[dl, "p"] \\
            & y
        \end{tikzcd}
    }
    In formulas, the compatibility type is
    ##{
        G(y)=\mathsf{ap}_f(\alpha)\cdot p.
    }

    By path induction on #{p:f(x)=y}, it suffices to treat
    #{y\equiv f(x)} and #{p\equiv\refl_{f(x)}}. The triangle becomes
    \graph{
        \begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large, row sep=large]
            {f(g(f(x)))}
                \arrow[rr, "{\mathsf{ap}_f(H(x))}"]
                \arrow[dr, "{G(f(x))}"']
            && {f(x)}
                \arrow[dl, "{\refl_{f(x)}}"] \\
            & {f(x)}
        \end{tikzcd}
    }
    We choose #{\alpha\defeq H(x):g(f(x))=x}. Its compatibility follows from
    the path calculation
    ##{
        \begin{aligned}
        G(f(x))
        &=\mathsf{ap}_f(H(x))
          &&\text{by the coherence }K(x) \\
        &=\mathsf{ap}_f(H(x))\cdot\refl_{f(x)}
          &&\text{by the right unit law.}
        \end{aligned}
    }
    Thus #{(H(x),K'(x))} gives an identity
    ##{
        (g(f(x)),G(f(x)))=(x,\refl_{f(x)})
    }
    in the fiber over #{f(x)}. Path induction transports this identity back to
    the original #{p:f(x)=y}, producing #{c_y=(x,p)}. Since #{(x,p)} was
    arbitrary, #{c_y} is a center of contraction. Since #{y} was arbitrary,
    every fiber of #{f} is contractible.
}
